            <div xml:id="d673">

                <pb facs="0897" n="795" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg795"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">765.84/1575: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-10-05T12:00:00+01:00">October 5,
                            1935—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received October 5—7:53 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>498. In reply to my question based on the Department’s 285, October 4, 6 p.m.,
                    Foreign Office stated that the attitude of British Government reported in my
                    493, October 4, 9 [<hi rendition="#italic">4?</hi>] p.m., “is based upon legal
                    considerations since no declaration of war exists”. Foreign Office added,
                    however, that the anticipated action of the Council over this week-end or Monday
                    would presumably alter the present status. Since this is all information
                    obtainable today I will again raise the question on Monday.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
